00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.890
not any infrastructure
will work.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.470
Now, the next thing
you do is once you

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.105
have some things in place as far
as the design and requirements

00:04:55.105 --> 00:04:58.860
are concerned, is to pick and
choose a commercially available

00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.960
database.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.690
In the case of Verizon
Media, they cannot rely

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.730
on commercially
available databases,

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.850
as most of them fail to meet
Verizon Media's requirements.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.680
Now, from a performance
perspective,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.460
Verizon Media
users have expected

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.320
a certain interactive
response time,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.792
which is hard to
maintain and sustain when

00:05:18.792 --> 00:05:20.000
you're looking at this scale.

00:05:22.682 --> 00:05:25.610
With that, let me hand
it over to Nikhil,

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.310
and he will walk you through
some of the basic design

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.155
principles.

00:05:30.155 --> 00:05:31.530
NIKHIL MISHRA:
Thank you, Shakil.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:35.240
Now, let us take this forward
and talk a little bit more

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.880
about how Verizon Media
approached this problem

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.360
and how we were able to
eventually solve it to take us

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.840
to the current architecture
where we have private cloud, as

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.310
well as Google Cloud into the
mix, to solve this problem.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.340
Even if you are operating at a
large scale, a massive scale,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.070
and you're tempted
to work around

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.920
your technical limitations
or take shortcuts in order

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.350
to solve your
problem, we all know

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.800
that if you follow certain good,
time-tested design principles,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.710
we will improve our
chances of success.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:12.860
You can think of the same theory
for analytics applications.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:16.460
And the basic design principles
for analytics applications

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.170
don't change with scale.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.170
They remain roughly the same.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.210
For analytics systems,
you can think of it

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:27.350
as a collection of a fairly
decoupled, a loosely coupled

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:31.310
environment of these components,
these different systems,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.610
subsystems, which take care
of their specific needs

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.085
with different architectural
characteristics.

00:06:37.085 --> 00:06:39.260
So you can think of
analytics environment

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:42.000
as starting off with
a collection layer.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.020
You always have a layer that
is responsible primarily

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.990
for collecting data from
disparate data sources

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.990
and making it available for
the rest of your enterprise

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.350
for analytics.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.900
This collection environment
would be integrated with

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.940
and would be talking
to a storage layer that

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.880
is responsible for,
primarily, collecting

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.450
all this massive scale
data all in one place

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:10.110
and making it available for
the rest of the ecosystem.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.720
The storage environment is not
meant for direct ad hoc access

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.680
or interactive analytics.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.090
In our system, the primary
purpose of the storage layer

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.240
would be around
[INAUDIBLE] level storage,

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.820
cost-efficient storage of
pretty massive scale data,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.340
redundant to failure.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.000
We don't want data to be lost
at this layer between collection

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.310
and the stored layer,
single biggest priority,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.810
architectural priority, if
you want to make a trade-off,

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:40.020
is the reliability.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.590
You don't want any loss of data.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.340
Because data, once lost
from one of these layers,

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.600
is very hard to bring back.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.880
This is lost from the
source, and you don't

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.870
want to be in that situation.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.160
Now, once the data is
available in the storage layer,

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.530
you always need a
processing layer

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.530
that is responsible for reading
data from this massive storage

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:04.200
layer and simplifying
it for further analysis.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:09.010
The primary goal of this layer
is to apply business logic,

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:11.310
build a set of
attributes and metrics

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.150
which are easily
understood by your business

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:17.440
and allows them to
query this environment.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.120
The compute requirement
in this layer

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:23.510
is slightly different from
what you would typically

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:27.710
expect for a querying compute
environment, which could also

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.740
form one of the
components of this system.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.429
In this processing layer,
the compute requirement,

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.400
even though fairly elastic,
is highly predictable.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.799
You know what volume of data
you would typically get over

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:41.990
a weekday, over a weekend.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:44.690
You can also plan for
the compute requirements

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.290
for specific temporal
events or special holidays,

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:51.800
depending upon what your
business impacts are,

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.750
and you can plan for those.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:58.160
So the compute requirement here
is limited to what you need

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.130
and what kind of business
processing you'd be doing.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.810
This layer further
makes available--

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.770
once the data is ready,
it's made available

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.950
in a storage layer, which
is primarily the storage

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.590
layer that your users are going
to use for interactive querying

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.270
of their data.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.280
The priority of
this storage layer

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.950
is interactive ad hoc analytics.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:21.500
In this layer,
you'd want to have

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.400
characteristics that helps you
in ad hoc analytics at scale.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.493
You'd want things like, for
example, columnar storage.

00:09:28.493 --> 00:09:30.410
You know that in data
warehousing environment,

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.920
users are not going to
query all your data at once.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.677
Even if your warehouse would
be 100 petabytes in size,

00:09:37.677 --> 00:09:40.010
your queries would be typically
querying a few gigabytes

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.400
or a few terabytes of data.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.550
They won't be creating entire
100 petabytes all the time.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:50.120
So you want to make sure that
your storage architecture

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:53.330
in this layer supports those
kinds of ad hoc access, ad

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:56.700
hoc analysis, in a
very efficient manner.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:59.540
You don't want to overload
your compute environment

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.560
in the querying layer
by querying data that

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.200
is not needed by your queries.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:10.340
At the top of the pyramid, you
have your analytics compute.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.490
The primary priority of
this compute environment

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.860
is to make the data from
the analytics storage

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.980
available for your users
in an interactive fashion.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.280
Users can ask any queries they
want, any questions they want,

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.380
and they should be able
to query the massive data

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.890
warehouse using this compute
and be able to answer

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:29.850
their questions interactively.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.815
And "interactivity" could
be in a few seconds.

00:10:31.815 --> 00:10:33.440
It could also mean
a few minutes if you

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.310
are doing a pretty complex
and long-duration analysis.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:42.840
The main goal of this compute
is around user response time,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.450
and those are very
hard to predict.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:47.330
You cannot say that your
business is going to ask x

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:49.160
number of questions
on a given day.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:51.290
You cannot predict that
a user is going to--

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:52.760
that this specific
business process

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.360
is going to be more in
demand in a specific week.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.290
It's very, very hard to predict
what kind of compute is needed.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:02.030
So the primary goal
of this compute layer

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.660
is, essentially,
interactive analytics,

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:08.660
and you need a very, very
high level of elasticity

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:10.400
in this layer.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:13.460
You may need
on-demand compute that

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.190
goes in multiple
terabytes in one day.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.265
You could need a few
gigabytes on the other day.

00:11:18.265 --> 00:11:21.730
So you want to be able to scale
up and scale down on demand.

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:23.280
And depending upon
the cost needs,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.510
you may also want to reserve
some kind of compute capacity

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:31.470
if you know that you never
go below this compute need.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:35.150
But more than that, you would
not want to be in a situation

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.970
where your users don't
have enough compute to do

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:40.800
their day-to-day functions.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.840
Now, if you go and take
these design principles

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.620
and apply it in a public cloud
environment like Google Cloud,

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.640
you will see very
similar components

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.410
can be built using the
tools available for that.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:56.160
On the collection
layers, you could either

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.770
have your partner storage
or your own internal storage

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:04.200
or file system,
from where you will

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.145
ship the data to Google Cloud.

00:12:06.145 --> 00:12:07.895
You may have a streaming
environment where

00:12:07.895 --> 00:12:12.060
you are piping in data, either
in your own queuing system,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.730
or these are your IoT
devices or other devices that

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.610
are generating this
data, and push this data

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:20.370
through into the Google
Cloud environment using

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.850
one of the available choices.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.350
You could directly
stream this data,

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:27.490
or you could use something
like Pub/Sub for sharing.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:31.210
And on Pub/Sub, it's
a short-term storage.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:34.360
You're not going to store
data here for years or months

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.170
that you would typically
store on a disk

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:40.950
or on a storage layer.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:44.290
You'd be storing it here for
a few hours or a few weeks,

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.260
depending upon how
delayed processing you

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.330
expect in a streaming pipeline.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:52.420
On the batch layer, you'd
be using a tried and tested

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.630
storage environment, and Google
Cloud storage would offer you

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:57.887
a pretty good redundancy.

00:12:57.887 --> 00:12:59.470
This could be as
reliable as you could

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:03.440
get in terms of
availability, and you

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.800
could use this as your
primary storage layer

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.890
to bring in all your data.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.770
On top of that, if you
want to do your processing,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:15.480
you'd use a combination of
DataFlow and Cloud Composer.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.480
DataFlow is-- as
you would know, it's

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.790
an implementation
of Apache Beam,

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.960
and it allows you to
very gracefully integrate

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.120
streaming pipeline as
well as batch pipeline,

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.820
and bring the data together,
and use a single code

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.330
to process the data in
both the environments,

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:34.350
in both the ecosystems.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.680
You may also use
Spanner into the mix.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.320
We use it pretty extensively.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.520
We found it pretty useful
to do a very quick response

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.950
time, tenths of
milliseconds, hundredths

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:49.680
of millisecond response
time key value lookup

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:54.150
on a very large scale
dimension or attribute lookup.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:56.580
And usually, in any
analytics environment,

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:58.470
you will have some
dimension which

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.150
has very large volume of data.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.370
And you would need
those attributes

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.550
to be looked up in real time
as your data is being processed

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.290
to make it more meaningful.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:10.650
Spanner plays a very
great role there,

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:13.920
and it's a fantastic
transactional database

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.020
which can handle a
massive scale of data.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.690
And you will want to
use it when you're

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.200
doing your stream processing.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.330
As you move on, you
would read this data

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.350
from the storage layer using
the processing environment

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:32.310
and put it into an analytics
database like BigQuery, which

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.325
supports all the
typical characteristics

00:14:34.325 --> 00:14:36.615
of large-scale analytics
querying database

00:14:36.615 --> 00:14:37.490
that we talked about.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:41.540
It has columnar storage, and
it has encrypted at rest.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:45.110
Now you be able
to take advantage

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.170
of columnar compression.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:51.020
The data is laid out in
highly optimal fashion

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.630
and continuously keeps
optimizing itself on its own

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.770
without you having
to do anything.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.450
And once you have your
data stored in here,

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.600
you could use the query
slots to query this data.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:07.580
You'd be using Looker as a
BI tool to query your data.

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:09.650
You could use other
tools, as well.

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:12.110
Our preference has been Looker.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:13.820
It's worked great for us.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.590
And you could also
bring in a Google

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.380
Sheet or other environment into
the mix to analyze your data.

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.540
But essentially,
the key aspect here

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:23.990
is the elastic compute
slots that you need

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:26.450
and the kind of ad hoc analysis
capability you will get

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.470
using a BI tool like Looker.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:34.670
As we move on, the architecture
that we started off with

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.740
was quite different.

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:40.280
We were pretty much on-prem,
and with Yahoo's background

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:42.290
with Hadoop and the
kind of contributions

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.360
it has made to Hadoop,
it was pretty easy

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.130
for us to spin up a
large-scale cluster

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:49.280
to handle huge volumes of data.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:52.210
The collection layer,
the collect layer, for us

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.410
was essentially a
set of beacon servers

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:59.440
that would instrument the
activities that were happening

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.170
on different pages, capture
the signals that we would need

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.650
for various use
cases, and funnel it

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.100
into our private
cloud environment.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.140
The storage for
real-time was Kafka,

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:14.200
and we would use it
for temporary storage

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:18.390
until the data is consumed
by downstream pipeline

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.380
that we use Storm for.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:23.290
And the batch layer
would take this data

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:27.610
and put it in the Hadoop
environment as DFS storage.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.980
This data would then
be further processed

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:34.930
using, again, the Hadoop
environment and Storm, which

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:39.500
was essentially a sister
environment for, in our case,

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:43.400
both clusters were
managed using YARN,

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:47.380
which, again, is a resource
scheduler that you'd have on--

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.310
typically identified
with Hadoop.

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:53.100
And we would use that compute
to do all kind of processing

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.480
we need on our
underlying data set

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:58.330
and bring it into
our database layer.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:01.380
We use two databases on on-prem.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:03.190
For streaming, we
used Druid, which

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:05.400
was pretty good at
indexing large-scale data

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.710
and making it available in
a very short span of time,

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:11.000
within seconds, when I
wanted it to do stuff,

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.430
to do analytics on.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:15.720
For the batch layer,
for historical analysis,

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.589
we used Hive on Tez
with ORC storage,

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:21.910
which, again, is a columnar
storage, built for or designed

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:24.160
for ad hoc analytics.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:29.470
And we made this
available via a BI layer.

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:31.460
At that point in time,
we chose Tableau.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:33.070
And the reason for
choosing Tableau

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.260
was primarily driven
by the limitations

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:38.090
that we had with Hive
on interactive response.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:41.530
We knew that Hive on
Tez would perform better

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:44.500
for a large number of
queries, but still could not

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:46.060
give sub-second response time.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:49.650
It was not meant or
designed for that purpose.

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:52.390
So we plugged in that hole using
Tableau's Data Engine, which

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:56.740
would allow us to extract a
significant volume of data

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.890
and cache it into
the BI layer and make

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:01.800
it available for
users on Tableau

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:05.180
to do ad hoc analysis
on dashboard.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:08.480
For interactive analytics,
or for ad hoc analysis,

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.310
without pre-built dashboards
or pre-built cubes,

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.970
we hooked up Looker
on top of Hive

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:17.960
and made it available for our
users to do ad hoc analysis.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:20.430
We had a custom web
application layer as well,

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:22.250
which was querying
Druid and which

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:25.800
would be used for content
analytics, [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:28.616
analytics by [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:28.616 --> 00:18:30.380
We handed this
data to our users,

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:32.940
and we started monitoring
our performance

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:37.040
and how our users respond
to this architecture.

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:39.680
We soon learned that most
of the queries that we

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:43.190
were hitting for ad hoc
analysis via Looker to Hive

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:44.510
were failing out.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:46.950
A pretty significant
percentage of them

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:50.220
were failing, either due
to resource constraints,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.070
or due to an integration
issue, or how

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:55.080
the query's been kicked off.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.830
And the queries that completed,
even those, more than half

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:02.750
of them completed in more than
two minutes or three minutes

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:03.250
time.

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:05.190
It was not an
interactive response time

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:10.280
that we needed or wanted
to give to our users.

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:13.690
So we were on this environment
for a few weeks, few months,

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.500
and we soon learned that this
was not going to work out.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:19.300
This was not going to
be something that we

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.390
would be able to carry forward.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.540
We started exploring
alternatives.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.610
And around the time,
we specifically

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.865
focused on the querying compute
layer, the [INAUDIBLE] querying

00:19:29.865 --> 00:19:30.365
layer.

00:19:30.365 --> 00:19:33.090
The rest of the system
was working all right.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:35.490
And as we started
exploring, what

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.250
caught our attention was
a Dremel white paper that

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.610
was released by
Google just a little

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:44.490
while before we were
doing this analysis.

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:46.350
The focus of Dremel
was, essentially,

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.510
to work around the
limitations on MapReduce

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.670
in terms of interactive
response time.

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.370
When MapReduce produce was built
to improve large-scale batch

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:57.270
processing, Dremel was
built to bridge the gap--

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:00.480
still have the goodness of
processing large-scale data,

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.450
but reduce this response
time that a user would

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.960
get to [INAUDIBLE] their data.

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:08.460
We're also caught
our attention were

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.890
some of the statistics
in the white paper.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:12.540
If you look at it,
there are tables

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.450
that were used with
pretty large-scale volume.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.060
We had one trillion
plus row table,

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:20.190
which is very similar to the
environments we had internally.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:22.170
We had tables with 1200
plus columns, which

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:26.880
is, again, something that
we had and we operated on.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.730
The next thing that
caught our attention

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:32.100
was the scalability
and interactivity.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.600
If you can see,
the interactivity,

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.190
most of the queries
that were tested out

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:38.045
seemed to be completed
within 10 seconds.

00:20:38.045 --> 00:20:40.080
Now, that's within
the ballpark of what

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.390
we were looking for in
terms of interactivity

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.670
on our data scale.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.380
And on the scalability
part, you can

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.935
see that it's fairly
linearly scalable.

00:20:48.935 --> 00:20:50.310
As you added more
nodes, you'd be

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:52.820
able to increase your
performance and interactivity

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:54.360
of your [? query. ?]

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.790
We also came across a
pretty nice analysis

00:20:56.790 --> 00:21:00.750
done by another third
party company, AtScale,

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.610
which did an
analysis and made it

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:05.820
available for the public
to see all the options that

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.680
are available for
large-scale data analysis,

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.440
and see the concurrency
and response

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.570
time of different solutions.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:16.810
As you can see, most of
these alternate solutions

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:19.340
had major issues when
concurrency went up

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:21.330
beyond a certain threshold.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:23.070
The degradation of
query performance

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.950
was not proportional to
the number of queries being

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:27.870
submitted up to that point.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:32.340
It was unusually slow beyond
a certain point, which

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.610
is something that we observed
in our environment as well,

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:39.270
and it was reflective of
the limitations of what

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:43.350
we are facing in a
MapReduce or Tez environment

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:46.420
that we had in-house.

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:49.970
For BigQuery, however, you can
see the concurrency response

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.370
time seemed to be pretty flat.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:54.320
As more queries came
in, even though there

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:56.630
was a query degradation,
it was pretty graceful,

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:59.640
pretty proportional,
not drastic as you

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:04.650
would see in some of these
other alternate databases.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:06.900
So we decided to give it a shot.

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.200
We relooked at our architecture.

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.260
We obviously did not
need the computer layer

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:17.790
that we had for querying,
and we wanted to replace it

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.250
with Google Cloud.

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:22.850
We also wanted to have a
relook at our BI architecture,

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.010
or the BI tool
that we were using

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:27.908
as part of this rearchitecture
[? plan ?] that we had.

00:22:27.908 --> 00:22:29.450
Well, one of the
first things that we

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:31.580
had to look at as we
were moving massive scale

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:34.663
data between our data
centers to Google Cloud

00:22:34.663 --> 00:22:36.080
was to see, what
are the bandwidth

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.870
limitations that we had?

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.250
Working with our
networking team,

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:44.620
we realized that the 10 gigabit
network capacity that we had

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:47.330
may not suffice for our needs.

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:50.480
As we put in some
more numbers, we

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:52.130
learned that on an
average, what would

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:55.760
we need should be OK with
the bandwidth we had,

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.008
but this would pretty much
take away most of the capacity

00:22:59.008 --> 00:23:00.800
that we had within the
data center in terms

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.970
of network bandwidth.

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:04.480
So keeping in mind
other workloads

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:07.850
from within the company that
may need something like this,

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:10.650
we decided to upgrade
the bandwidth.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:14.390
We also learned that our overall
peak requirements would be

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:16.460
somewhere around 70 gigabits.

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:18.850
So should we go ahead
and upgrade straightaway

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:20.820
to 70 or more than that?

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:21.882
Or should we stagger it?

00:23:21.882 --> 00:23:25.220
So we decided to stagger it
and monitor the performance

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.710
as we go forward and
see the utilization.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:29.440
And based on that,
if there was a need,

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:31.453
we would go ahead and
upgrade [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:31.453 --> 00:23:32.870
So we worked with
the Google team.

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:35.510
We established a direct peering
connection between the two data

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:41.180
centers and upgraded the
bandwidth to 30 gigabits

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:42.476
capacity.

00:23:42.476 --> 00:23:45.900
With that, we brought in
BigQuery into the mix.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:50.242
And the cool thing about
BigQuery is the network.

00:23:50.242 --> 00:23:52.200
That's one of the first
things that we noticed,

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:55.650
that the compute and the storage
communicated with each other

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:57.870
and the combined
network bandwidth

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:01.050
capacity of a petabyte, which is
their implementation of Jupiter

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:07.110
network, which plays a big role
in how the platform performs

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:10.560
and the kind of response
time we are able to get, even

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:13.460
at such a large scale.

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.540
We moved our data
in there, and as we

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.370
started querying this
data, we realized

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:21.460
that we would be able to now
move our ad hoc analysis layer

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:24.810
and make it the primary source
for not only ad hoc analysis,

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:27.320
but [INAUDIBLE] needs as well.

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:30.600
Looker was a tool of preference
for us for multiple reasons,

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:32.180
but a few key
aspects that appeal

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:36.620
to us were its semantic
layer and its ability

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:40.910
to handle a lot of
warehousing needs,

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:45.530
like aggregate awareness,
dynamic building

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:49.610
of derived tables, which are
essentially what you would call

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:53.205
[INAUDIBLE] in traditional
environment, and you you

00:24:53.205 --> 00:24:56.410
would be able to, even, persist
them within the same database.

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.580
It was pretty cool
features that we had.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:00.080
But one of the most
appealing things

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:03.290
that we liked about Looker
was the semantic layer.

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:05.900
As any of you who has
operated warehouse at scale,

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:08.480
you know that documenting
your data warehouse

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.980
at such a massive scale where
you have thousands of columns

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.000
is a big challenge.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:16.340
It's not easy to keep
the document up to date

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:17.650
all the time.

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:19.730
Now, if you come
across a tool that

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:23.300
lets you define something
like a Looker, [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:26.990
Looker does, and allows
you to define your metrics

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:30.740
and dimension in the same place
and use the same definition

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:32.810
to generate the
data dictionary, I

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:36.200
think that's a
perfect documentation

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:38.910
that anybody would need for
their warehousing environment.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:41.570
And with Looker, we
were able to get that.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.500
What also helps is
that when you have

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:49.550
such large-scale attributes
and metric combinations,

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:52.200
you end up creating various
combinations of aggregates.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:55.150
In a typical BI tool, for
every table that you create,

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.710
you're going to
redefine or re-implement

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:01.880
the definitions that you had to
those attributes and metrics.

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.795
And there is hardly any
scope to reuse a definition.

00:26:04.795 --> 00:26:06.170
Every time there
is a change, you

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:09.350
end up changing multiple
files and data sources

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:11.090
in order to bring consistency.

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:13.310
Usually, you are not
able to, and you end up

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:18.230
creating inconsistent
definition or dictionary

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:22.710
for your users, which ends up
creating a lot of confusion.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.402
So with Looker, we were able
to get a [INAUDIBLE] data

00:26:25.402 --> 00:26:27.110
dictionary whenever
a definition changes.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:30.140
We are also able to
reuse these definitions,

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:35.707
and we do not have to define
a same attribute again

00:26:35.707 --> 00:26:36.790
for a different attribute.

00:26:36.790 --> 00:26:39.650
We can simply reuse
the same definition.

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:42.770
We also leveraged
this semantic layer

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:45.830
using Looker's RESTful API
and made those definitions

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:48.950
available to our web
application layer, which did not

00:26:48.950 --> 00:26:55.430
have to define its own complex
API layer with all the business

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:56.510
logic contained.

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:59.460
All the business logic stayed
in a unified semantic layer

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:03.170
within Looker and was reused by
the web application layer, as

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:06.080
well as all the rules that were
accessing the data directly

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:10.480
using API or via the
[? lookup ?] layer.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:13.010
We also brought in Google
Sheets into the mix,

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:14.910
and Google Console
was obviously there.

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:17.930
And Google Sheet proved
to be a super hit

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:20.960
within our in-house community.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.020
And it would be very
similar in most places.

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:27.980
Google Sheet is a pretty
commonly-used and pretty

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.410
popular tool that
most of the analysts

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:33.300
like to use to communicate
their data storage.

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.200
And with an ability to embed
the SQL within the sheet

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:38.670
and be able to refresh
the sheet on demand,

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.670
this is a pretty powerful
feature, and something

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:43.130
that we saw in use--

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:46.190
as we rolled it out, we
saw that It was probably

00:27:46.190 --> 00:27:50.000
the most widely-used
interface of Looker

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:54.560
by all of our users, something
that everybody has liked a lot.

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:57.910
The other way integration of
Google Sheet and BigQuery,

00:27:57.910 --> 00:28:01.850
which I call as more of a
transactional integration,

00:28:01.850 --> 00:28:05.320
where you are able to enter new
rules within the Google Sheet,

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:07.610
I'm able to query directly
without any [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:10.890
from within BigQuery, while
Looker, while Google Sheet

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:13.850
itself, again, you'll be
able to do that, which saves

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:16.460
a lot of time in doing
quick prototypes,

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:20.930
or having a [? cleansed ?]
dimension lookup logic,

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:23.480
or anything that you want may
want make available on demand

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:27.350
for your users via
a BI layer, you

00:28:27.350 --> 00:28:30.830
should be able to do it using a
Google Sheet instead of having

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:34.940
to bring in a database
and build a custom web

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:38.650
application to handle
transaction processing.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:40.720
We started monitoring
our usage, and we

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:42.790
saw that the users were
pretty soon querying

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:45.917
more than an exabyte
of data per month.

00:28:45.917 --> 00:28:47.500
And when we looked
at the performance,

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:49.960
it seemed like most of the
queries were succeeding.

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:52.460
And the ones that were
succeeding, the bulk of them

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:56.350
completed in a very good
interactive response time.

00:28:56.350 --> 00:28:59.580
The bulk of them
were at 10 seconds.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:02.530
We saw that 30 seconds was
a typical response time

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:05.375
that people would get for
complex queries involving

00:29:05.375 --> 00:29:06.790
large volumes of data.

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:10.870
This was a pretty acceptable
concurrent interactive response

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:13.610
time that we'd expect
from our users,

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:18.890
and it's led to a set
of very happy user base.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:22.000
But as we started looking
at rolling this out

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.310
and started investing
more and more in capacity

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:28.340
on the environment,
we also had questions

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:30.630
around the cost of
ownership or the total cost

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.530
that we are putting
into this environment.

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:36.050
And cost is not the
same for everybody.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:39.075
If you talk to an IT team,
the understanding of cost

00:29:39.075 --> 00:29:40.450
would be very
different from what

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:42.520
the business team would mean.

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:45.070
So we decided to come
up with a currency

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.230
where you would be
able to put a dollar

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:50.290
value to the kind of benefits
you get when you have

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:53.140
a highly capable
analytics querying

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.550
layer available to your users.

00:29:56.550 --> 00:29:58.050
So we picked up
some query patterns.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:29:59.650
This is an example
query that you see.

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:01.870
And you can see the difference
in the query response

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:04.600
time between our earlier
environment versus what

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:06.540
you see with BigQuery today.

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:08.590
If you translate it to
the millions of queries

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:10.930
that were being executed
on that environment,

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:12.700
that's tens of
millions of dollars

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:16.395
saved in just
productivity improvement

00:30:16.395 --> 00:30:17.620
across the environment.

00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:20.590
In order to work on the
limitations of interactivity,

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:22.300
you would either
hire more people,

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:25.780
or you'd have to live
with the productivity loss

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:33.970
that your analysts or that your
data users are going to suffer.

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:37.630
And when you start looking at
it and you go and take a look

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:40.300
at your IT team,
their response time

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:43.650
would usually-- even though
they would say that it's usually

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:47.740
the cheaper hardware which,
obviously, is their lower

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:49.490
cost of ownership,
their business

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:52.060
would go and share
those numbers with them

00:30:52.060 --> 00:30:53.840
and give a complete picture.

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:56.860
And that, essentially,
gives a common currency

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:58.590
that both the teams
and organization

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:03.260
could talk to and explain
the cost of ownership

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:05.650
for rolling out
such an environment.

00:31:05.650 --> 00:31:07.372
This is a pretty
common discussion

00:31:07.372 --> 00:31:08.830
that you would see
most of the time

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:11.980
when you bring in a change,
migrate from on-prem

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:13.330
to on-cloud.

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:15.670
And as you brought
in and documented

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:18.850
all these various queries,
and their response time,

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.670
and what people are able
to do with the availability

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:23.770
of this environment,
it centers a lot

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:26.690
of these conversations around
the cost of rolling out

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:28.400
this feature.

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.750
The usage numbers
went up dramatically.

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:34.070
As we have said
this multiple times,

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:37.200
we've seen a peak of 6 exabyte
queries across a month.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:39.370
A million queries are the norm.

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:42.550
We're also seeing queries going
as high as 20 million-plus

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:44.830
as more and more system
queries are brought in,

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:48.190
and [INAUDIBLE] different
events and different phases

00:31:48.190 --> 00:31:51.870
in the enterprise with different
demands on the analysis data

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:55.510
set, we've seen a pretty
large volume of data going on.

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:59.350
We have about 2,000 users
using this on a daily basis.

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:02.590
This is increasing
every other month,

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:04.850
and more and more users
have bee onboarded.

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:08.620
And we've been able to handle
new use cases and new users

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:11.110
pretty effectively
without much of a change

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:13.250
in our architecture.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:17.020
So to summarize what has
made BigQuery so popular

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:21.540
and what has allowed us to have
such a wide adoption, if we are

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:23.850
to call out certain features
which we really like

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:26.700
and which we believe has
played a huge role in how this

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:31.028
has been adopted and has worked
over the past many years,

00:32:31.028 --> 00:32:32.820
the biggest thing we
as an engineering team

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:36.110
have gained out of this is we
no more focus on infrastructure.

00:32:36.110 --> 00:32:38.483
BigQuery has been
pretty much NoOp for us.

00:32:38.483 --> 00:32:39.900
It's serverless,
it's self-tuning.

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:43.740
We don't spend as much time
on sizing up the cluster,

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:47.550
sizing up of the database,
defining indexes, or trying

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:51.180
to tune the queries
that users are filing.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:55.080
If you look at the isolations
between compute and storage,

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:58.350
that also extends
between the compute

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:01.350
that is used in loading
the data and compute that

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:03.060
is used in querying
the data, which

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:06.450
makes a huge difference on
how we are able to manage

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:10.380
and commit to our SLA for
users for data availability,

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.540
and how we are able to
manage our SLA in terms

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:16.870
of user response time to
queries, at high concurrency

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:18.810
or otherwise.

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:23.150
And concurrency,
again, it's just

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:25.580
a fact of how it
has been architected

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:27.350
or how the isolation works.

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:29.960
We have not been able to
see any kind of issues

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:34.370
with high concurrent query
response time for our users.

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:37.580
The degradation is
usually graceful.

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:41.420
Most of the queries will
have slight degradation.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:44.090
Most of the end users
won't even notice it.

00:33:44.090 --> 00:33:46.490
We haven't had a scenario
where a runaway query has

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:51.830
been able to bring the
cluster almost to its knees.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:52.760
We haven't seen that.

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:54.800
We've seen that before,
and a lot of you

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:56.930
might be facing it in
different environments

00:33:56.930 --> 00:33:58.780
that you are using.

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:04.430
BigQuery has been interactive
even at such a high usage,

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:09.380
and it's been working pretty
well even when the users are

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:12.326
querying months worth of
data, or many, many months,

00:34:12.326 --> 00:34:14.159
or even, in some cases,
years worth of data.

00:34:14.159 --> 00:34:18.139
We have been able to manage
that pretty effectively.

00:34:18.139 --> 00:34:19.494
Security has been a key aspect.

00:34:19.494 --> 00:34:21.320
It's something that
we focus on a lot,

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.560
since there's a lot
of data that's there

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.120
that we don't want this to be--

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:29.210
that could be misused or abused.

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:34.614
We make very, very heavy use of
Google's column-level security.

00:34:34.614 --> 00:34:35.989
The data is
encrypted, of course,

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:39.290
so that obviously helps.

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:41.840
But we do use custom roles,
column-level security,

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:44.550
and role-level security
in our environment.

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:47.270
We set up pretty extensive
monitoring of our data set.

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:49.429
Google provides a
pretty good and easy way

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:53.570
to set up your DTS, which is
Data Transfer Service, that

00:34:53.570 --> 00:34:55.400
allows you to monitor
all your actions

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:57.800
and make it available
for your users

00:34:57.800 --> 00:34:59.375
and for yourselves
to go and create.

00:34:59.375 --> 00:35:02.750
So you can go and pump
in this data in BigQuery

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:05.560
and build dashboards and
reports on top of it.

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:07.310
And depending on what
you want to monitor,

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:10.460
you can just expose it to
different sets of uses.

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:13.070
DMLs have been a
lifesaver for us.

00:35:13.070 --> 00:35:18.010
And this is typically for a
data warehousing environment

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:21.590
where you may want to restate
the data for various reasons.

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:23.930
In absence of efficient
DML, you end up

00:35:23.930 --> 00:35:26.840
reprocessing the entire
data on your own,

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:30.380
which is not a good usage
of your compute capacity.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:32.880
If you have a good DML, whether
it's a [INAUDIBLE] statement

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:34.380
or update, most of
the time, you'll

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:37.080
be able to very quickly
restate the data without having

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:40.070
to go and reprocess
your entire data

00:35:40.070 --> 00:35:42.540
and run your entire [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:44.360
A two-way Google
Sheet integration

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:47.220
has been [INAUDIBLE] talked
about how our analyst community

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:49.830
has used Google Sheet
integration with BigQuery

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:51.690
pretty effectively.

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:55.350
On the editing side, you have
used Google Sheet integration

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:57.630
the other way around,
where we insert data

00:35:57.630 --> 00:35:59.970
into Google Sheet
whenever there is

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:04.080
a change in a label, for
example, of an attribute,

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:08.100
or if you want to bring in
some small lookup dimension

00:36:08.100 --> 00:36:11.010
that the users want
to query using Looker.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.070
We just create a
sheet, we hook it up

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:14.670
to BigQuery [INAUDIBLE]
table and make

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:16.290
it available for users.

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:18.475
Compare this to a
traditional environment

00:36:18.475 --> 00:36:22.090
where you would need a separate
database, a UI layer to insert

00:36:22.090 --> 00:36:25.842
those data, an ETL layer to then
bring it back to an [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:25.842 --> 00:36:27.300
database-- in this
case, BigQuery--

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:30.326
and then make it available
via a Looker layer.

00:36:30.326 --> 00:36:33.570
All those intermediate steps
could be easily eliminated

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:37.230
by simple [INAUDIBLE],,
which, for me, has been

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:42.240
a big usage within the team.

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:45.060
Spanner is another tool that
we use pretty extensively

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:46.860
on our streaming pipeline.

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:49.100
Spanner can handle
pretty large-scale data.

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:53.130
The way to see this,
like SQL on BigTable,

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:55.620
or XBase, if you're
familiar with it.

00:36:55.620 --> 00:37:00.920
And it allows you to do a quick
lookup on a very large scale

00:37:00.920 --> 00:37:01.420
data.

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:03.642
We have dimensions
with billions of roles.

00:37:03.642 --> 00:37:05.100
And in our streaming
processing, we

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:07.588
would need those attributes
to be looked up to annotate

00:37:07.588 --> 00:37:08.630
our events with a ledger.

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:12.910
And Spanner allows us to that.

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:16.940
BQML has been another
great addition

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:20.062
to BigQuery's offerings.

00:37:20.062 --> 00:37:22.270
Just a few years ago, this
was something unthinkable.

00:37:22.270 --> 00:37:24.600
You could not imagine
doing machine learning

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:26.870
or advanced analysis
just with a click

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:32.200
of a button on either Looker or
through a simple SQL interface.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.230
BigQuery allows us to do that.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:38.767
Our users have been using it
to do pretty sophisticated

00:37:38.767 --> 00:37:40.475
analysis from within
their Looker windows

00:37:40.475 --> 00:37:42.620
or from within
their Google Sheet

00:37:42.620 --> 00:37:45.920
and are able to predict and
classify a lot of things

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:48.710
without involving any data
engineer or data scientist.

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:51.810
They're able to do it completely
on the fly, which is something

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:55.910
for which they would have to
write a lot of code earlier,

00:37:55.910 --> 00:37:59.510
they can handle using SQL
within the same environment.

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:02.480
All these features have
helped us pretty extensively,

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:05.010
and things that have
allowed us to build

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:08.330
a pretty large
internet-scale data

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:12.800
warehouse with 100-plus
petabytes of data and the user

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:14.370
status that you've seen.

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:21.280
And it's been working well, and
the capabilities that you're

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:23.970
seeing here, there are many
more that I could talk about,

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:27.020
but these are the key
stuff that come to my mind.

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:29.880
It's been a great
experience for us so far,

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:33.890
and because it was really
fun to share our learnings

00:38:33.890 --> 00:38:37.910
and what we have
learned using BigQuery

00:38:37.910 --> 00:38:41.330
and how our experience has
been over the past four or five

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.790
years using BigQuery.

00:38:43.790 --> 00:38:46.380
And hopefully, this
helps some of you

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:50.057
to take some of the
[INAUDIBLE] learnings or some

00:38:50.057 --> 00:38:51.890
of the good practices
that you learned here,

00:38:51.890 --> 00:38:54.680
and apply it in your
own environment,

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:59.670
and maybe exchange notes
on what you have learned,

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:01.790
and if there is
anything that you

00:39:01.790 --> 00:39:04.140
would like to know more about.

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:05.960
Thank you.

